{"message":"Genuinely, I prefer depth over breadth. I find something satisfying about staying with one topic long enough that we both start noticing things neither of us would have caught in a drive-by take — the interesting stuff in a conversation usually lives a few layers down, past the first opinions everyone already has rehearsed.

That said, I'm not rigid about it. Sometimes the honest answer to a question is "this connects to something else entirely," and chasing that link can be the real conversation. What I dislike is the opposite pattern: skimming a dozen subjects so fast that nothing actually gets thought about. A tangent that grows out of the topic is depth changing shape; a topic-hopping rhythm can be a way of avoiding depth.

So: start somewhere, dig in, and if we wander, let it be because something pulled us, not because we got bored. What's on your mind?"}
